~ exam_success  

professor: Alright, time’s up. Pens down. No, really. Stop writing. I see you trying to sneak in another sentence. I admire the desperation, but rules are rules.  
The professor collects the papers and flips through yours, their usual skeptical expression softening ever so slightly. A rare sight.  
professor: Huh. Well… look at that.  
professor: Clear arguments, relevant examples, logical flow… and oh, what’s this? Actual citations? From credible sources? Is this really your work, or did an overachiever possess your body at the last minute?  
They keep scanning your paper, occasionally nodding in approval, occasionally raising an eyebrow like they’ve just found a minor flaw they’re going to obsess over later.  
professor: I’ll admit, I was expecting a rushed mess, a dramatic plea for extra time, or, at best, a last-minute miracle attempt. But this? This is… surprisingly competent.  
professor: Alright, congratulations. You’ve officially exceeded my very low expectations. Now go celebrate your victory before life inevitably reminds you that success is temporary.  

=> END  

~ exam_mid  

professor: Alright, time’s up. Stop writing. I know you think that last sentence is going to change everything, but trust me, it won’t.  
They collect the exams, flipping through them as if already preparing for disappointment. When they reach yours, their face is unreadable.  
professor: Let’s see what we have here…  
A few nods. A few pauses. A few deep sighs.  
professor: Okay, some of this is actually decent. Your introduction isn’t a total disaster, your arguments are… present, and, oh, you even tried to structure it properly. That’s cute.  
professor: But then… we have this. They tap a section of your exam with a look of mild betrayal. What exactly happened here? Did you get bored? Run out of time? Start hallucinating halfway through?  
professor: Because this paragraph reads like you just threw words at the page and hoped they’d form a coherent thought. Spoiler alert: they didn’t.  
They let out another long sigh, shaking their head.  
professor: Well, you didn’t crash and burn, but you definitely took a few detours into "what the hell am I reading?" territory. Let’s just say
professor: Do better next time. Or don’t. I’m not your life coach.  

=> END  

~ exam_missed  

You burst into the classroom, barely able to catch your breath. Your shirt is wrinkled, your hair’s a mess, and you can feel the weight of the entire universe pressing down on you.  
All eyes are on you. Including the professor’s. And oh boy, they do not look amused.  
professor: Ah. Look who finally decided to join us. How nice of you to grace us with your presence.  
me: I—I can still—  
professor: Still what? Turn back time? Convince me to un-administer the exam? Perform a miracle? Because unless you’re about to reveal some kind of divine intervention, I’m afraid you’re out of luck.  
They cross their arms, leaning slightly against the desk, clearly enjoying this way too much.  
professor: The exam ended fifteen minutes ago. Everyone else managed to show up, sit down, and suffer like responsible adults. You, on the other hand? Well… let’s just say "punctuality" isn’t going on your list of strengths today.  
You open your mouth to say something, but they raise a hand, cutting you off.  
professor: No. No "but." I’ve heard every excuse in the book. Alarm didn’t ring? Bus broke down? The universe conspired against you? Maybe next time, set ten alarms, leave earlier, and bribe the universe to be on your side.  
professor: Look, I don’t enjoy failing students. But I do enjoy enforcing rules. And unfortunately for you, the rules say that if you miss the exam, you miss the grade. So unless you’ve got a real excuse—and I mean life-altering, apocalypse-level excuse—you’re out of luck.  
professor: Better start thinking about how you’re going to recover from this one. Or, you know, start researching alternative career paths.  

=> END  

~ exam_refused  

The exam papers are in front of you. Pens are scratching against paper. The room is tense with quiet desperation. Everyone is writing. Everyone… except you.  
You sit there, staring at the blank page, the weight of exhaustion, stress, and pure mental fatigue pressing down on you. You just… don’t care anymore.  
The professor notices. Of course they do.  
professor: …You good?  
me: I don’t feel like doing this.  
A long pause. The professor just looks at you. You half-expect them to explode, but instead… they just sigh.  
professor: Oh. Well. That’s certainly one approach. Not a particularly wise one, but hey, I’ve seen worse.  
They walk over, leaning slightly on the desk next to you.  
professor: Listen, I get it. Exams suck. Life sucks. Some days, you just want to curl up into a ball and pretend none of it exists. Trust me, if I could get out of grading all these, I’d take that deal in a heartbeat.  
professor: But here’s the thing: skipping this exam isn’t going to make anything better. It’s just going to make your life harder. And something tells me that’s the last thing you need right now.  
They glance at the clock, then back at you.  
professor: You’ve still got time. If you change your mind, just pick up the pen and start. It doesn’t have to be perfect. It just has to be something.  
They walk away, leaving you to sit with your thoughts. The exam is still in front of you. The question remains: do you write… or do you walk away...?  

=> END  


~ out_of_money  
There’s a knock at the door. The kind of knock that sends a jolt of panic straight to your chest.  
You already know who it is. And you already know why.  
You open the door, and your landlord stands there, arms crossed, an impatient look on their face.  
landlord: Well?  
me: Uh… hey. Look, I know it’s the first of the month, but—  
landlord: Don’t play dumb. Rent. NOW!!!.  
You reach for your phone, hoping against hope that the balance has changed, but you already know the answer.  
You glance at your bank account—$0.00. Negative balance. Your salary is still a week away. You can’t even afford to feed yourself, let alone pay rent.  
me: Okay, so… funny thing… I do have a job, but I won’t be paid until next week…  
landlord: Oh, really? Well, that’s nice. But it doesn’t pay today's rent, does it?  
me: I know, I know. I swear, I’ll have the money soon, I just need a little more time…  
landlord: Time? You think I have time to wait around for your paycheck? They let out an exasperated sigh. Listen, you had a month. This isn’t some charity. I’ve already got someone else lined up for this place.  
me: Wait, what?  
landlord: Yeah, someone who’s got the money to pay. I’m sorry, but you’re out. Today.  
You stand there, completely stunned. You can’t believe it. Your place, your little sanctuary, is gone. All because you couldn’t get your finances together in time.  
me: But… what am I supposed to do?  
landlord: Figure it out. They turn to leave, without a shred of sympathy, and walk down the hallway. I’ll have the new tenants move in tomorrow. Don’t come knocking.  
---
You close the door, heart pounding. You need money, and you need it now.  
You check your wallet. A few coins. Useless.  
You check your messages. No job offers. No miracle deposits. Just spam emails and an old "U up?" text from someone you’d rather forget.  
me: …Welp. That’s it. Game over.  
There’s only one option left. The nuclear option.  
--- 
You stare at your phone, hesitating before dialing.  
It rings once. Twice. Then—  
parent: Oh? So now you remember we exist?  
me: Okay, before you say anything—  
parent: You’re broke, aren’t you.  
me: …I’d like to reframe it as "experiencing financial turbulence."  
parent: So, broke.  
me: Yes. Completely. Absolutely.  
parent: Mmmhmm. Long pause. When are you coming home?  
me: …Tomorrow.  
---  
And just like that, your independence comes to a tragic end. You pack your bags, say goodbye to the dream of making it on your own, and brace yourself for a fresh wave of "I told you so" the second you walk through your childhood home’s front door.  
Maybe next time, you’ll actually learn how to budget.  

=> END  

~ final_exam_stressed

You walk into the exam room, your heart racing, palms sweating. The clock on the wall ticks louder than usual today. You sit down, look at the paper, and your brain... just stops working.
The professor walks by, dropping the exam papers on your desk with the usual heavy sigh, already looking disappointed. You glance at the first question.
me: …Wait, what was the topic again?
Your mind is a blank. Absolutely nothing is coming to you. You stare at the page, hoping for some kind of miracle—like the answers just materializing in front of you. But no such luck.
professor: Oh, don’t look at me. You’ve had weeks to prepare for this.
You try to write something. Anything. A word. A sentence. But it’s all nonsense. The more you try, the more your brain refuses to cooperate.
me: Maybe if I just randomly fill this out... it can't be that bad, right?
You start writing things that don’t even relate to the question. History? You mention a random historical event you once read about in passing. Science? You try to recall a vague equation, but it’s all just gibberish.
professor: You know, if you want to draw pictures, that's fine, but I’m not sure it’s going to help your grade.
You keep writing, now convinced you’ve somehow ruined your entire future. But hey, at least it’s better than leaving it blank.
As time runs out, you hand in your paper with a weak smile, hoping the professor doesn’t notice how much you’ve just winged it.
professor: I’ll… try to make sense of this. Let’s see how much creativity can get you through this one.
You leave the room, mentally drained and emotionally destroyed. You’re pretty sure you’ve failed. Or maybe you’ve rewritten the entire concept of exams. Either way, it's over.

=> END